# CVPR 2027 terminology consistency

The AAAI submission was criticised for wording that did not match between metric definitions, tables, code and prose ("Defination", undefined "false-cross", Swap-accuracy "higher is better" when lower was meant, FPR vs false activation vs false cross used interchangeably). Do not repeat this.

**Single source of truth**: the Glossary section in `doc/CVPR2027_GATE_LOG.md`. Before introducing or renaming any metric, outcome, probe or gate name anywhere (Python identifiers, JSON keys, CLI flags, log fields, Markdown, LaTeX), check the glossary and use that exact term. If a new term is needed, add it to the glossary in the same change.

Rules:
- One name per concept. Use the glossary's canonical name, not a synonym (e.g. `stale_leak`, never "own-expert false positive" or "self leak").
- Code identifiers, JSON keys and paper terms must map 1:1. Snake_case in code (`total_leak`), the same words in prose ("total leak"), never a third variant in tables.
- Every metric states its direction once, in the glossary, and tables carry the same arrow (lower is better / higher is better). Never describe a lower-is-better metric as an improvement when it goes up.
- Do not reuse a term with a different meaning than it had in the AAAI draft without an explicit note in the glossary row ("replaces AAAI 'false activation rate'").
- Probe names are exactly: `reliability`, `image_swap`, `hard_negative`, `modality_consistency`, `prompt_conflict`, `image_rephrase`, `image_loc`, `text_loc`. Never "swap probe" vs "image swap" vs "swapped-image" interchangeably in the same document.
- Gate names are `G0`..`G6`, `G2b`; methodology-change items are `M1`, `M2`, ...
- Spelling: American English, consistent across files ("artifact" not "artefact"; "definition" not "defination").

When editing paper text, docs or result tables, re-read the glossary first and flag (do not silently change) any existing wording that conflicts with it.
